The Minix library for the Stackette
===================================

build.sh compiles lib/{ansi,posix,other,stdio,syslib,math,fphook}, assembles
lib/syscall (the stubs are portable as they are) and the machine dependent
files of this directory, and makes libc.a, libsys.a, libm.a and crtso.o:

    ACK=/path/to/ack/.obj/staging OUT=/tmp/minixlib sh build.sh

The machine dependent files:

  sendrec.s   _send, _receive, _sendrec: traps 1, 2, 3 of the kernel
  setjmp.s    setjmp, _setjmp, _kjmp: setjmp and longjmp are traps 32 and 33
  setjmp.c    the signal mask part of setjmp, longjmp, _longjmp
  brksize.s   the initial break
  crtso.s     the start of a process (argc, argv, envp from the stack image)

Not written yet: ___sigreturn (it depends on the signal frame that the kernel
builds), getprocessor and the kernel itself.

test/run.sh builds a program against the library and runs it on the Python model of
the CPU, with test/harness.c in place of the kernel. tools-stackette/retcheck.py
cross-checks the sizes of the results and the arguments of all calls in
a set of EM files (compile with `ack -mstackette -c.k`): on the Stackette the
callee removes the arguments and leaves the result, so a function that is
declared with one type and defined with another unbalances the stack.

Overlays (tools-stackette/ovlprep.py, ovltest/). The code space of a process is
64K. A program that needs more has a root of 48K, always mapped, and overlays of
16K, one of which is mapped at a time in the window at 0xc000. The functions
of an overlay are put in an own section (.ovl0, .ovl1, ...), from the
assembly files of the compiler (`ack -mstackette -c.s`), with a stub in the
root for each: the stub has the name of the function and is a `trap $5` with
the overlay, the address in the window and the address of the stub of the
return (trap $6). The kernel (mpx.c) keeps the return address of the call and the
overlay of the caller in the frames of the process (8 of them), maps the
overlay of the callee in the window and goes on in the function; the return stub
maps the caller's overlay again. The calls inside an overlay and to the root go
straight. The image of the text is the root padded to 48K and the overlays padded
to 16K, ack2minix.py makes it, MM reads it in pieces of 16K and the kernel maps
the root and the overlay of the window (more than 16 pages of text mean overlays).
A static function whose address is taken (a callback) gets a stub like the global ones.
